] "\n" [:option {:value "920003"} "Painting, Open"] "\n" [:option {:value "920004"} "Sculpturing, Open"]] [:script {} "$('#same_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/results/' + $('#same_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:form {:class "form-inline form-inline-header"} [:select {:name "id", :id "other_games_select", :class "form-control"} [:option {:value ""} "In other Games"] "\n" [:option {:value "920006"} "1920"] "\n" [:option {:value "920011"} "1924"] "\n" [:option {:value "920033"} "1932"] "\n" [:option {:value "6000245"} "1952"]] [:script {} "$('#other_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/results/' + $('#other_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:a {:href "/event_names/715"} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "Literature, Open" [:small {} [:a {:class "footnote", :href "#", :title "No silver and bronze medals awarded.", :data-toggle "tooltip", :data-placement "right"} "1"]]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} " 5 May – 27 July 1912"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/241"} "Karlavägen 10, Stockholm"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/920001"} "7 from 4 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The gold medal for literature was awarded to “Georges Hohrod et M. Eschbach, Germany”, for the work “Ode to Sport,” which was submitted in three languages - French, English, and German.  But in reality, the poetic ode had been written by " [:a {:href "/athletes/920613"} "Pierre, Baron de Coubertin"] ", who entered it in the competitions under the dual pseudonym.  Coubertin had used the name Georges Hohrod previously, publishing an autobiographic novel, " [:strong {} "Le Roman d’un rallié"] ", under that pseudonym in 1899.  It is believed that M. Eschbach was his wife, Marie (née Rothan), who grew up partly in Germany, and that she translated the work into German."] "\n\n" [:p {} "But what of the names Hohrod and Eschbach?  Until recently, the source of the names was not known.  But an article by Jean Durry in " [:em {} "Olympic Review"] " has shed light on the mystery.  Durry attempted to find the home in Luttenbach that had once belonged to the Rothan family, Coubertin’s in-laws. Driving in the Fecht Valley near Alsace, Durry came across the small town of Hohrodberg.  Very near Hohrodberg, Durry also found the small town of Eschbach-au-Val. Although he does not describe it in as much detail, this was noted earlier (1994) by Norbert Müller, who wrote in a footnote in his book on Olympic Congresses, “The author of this book discovered that the pseudonyms Hohrod and Eschbach were the names of two neighboring villages of the native village of Coubertin’s wife, Luttenbach, near Colmar”."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Catalogue Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Translated Title"] [:th {} "Original Title"] [:th {} "Title in Olympic Report or Catalogue"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "–"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/920613"} " Georges Hohrod, Martin Eschbach"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GER"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GER.png"}] "GER"]] [:td {} "Ode to Sport"] [:td {} "Ode au Sport / Ode an den Sport"] [:td {} "Ode au Sport / Ode an den Sport"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {} [:a {:class "footnote", :href "#", :title "The pseudonym of IOC President Pierre de Coubertin", :data-toggle "tooltip", :data-placement "right"} "2"]]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} [:span {:class "label label-primary"} [:abbrev {:title "Also Competed"} "AC"]]] [:td {:class "bib"} "–"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/920848"} "Gabriele D'Annunzio"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/ITA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vert